<p>While there is more context here, the concept still comes across a bit flat as there isn't much to get invested in. Currently the skip still just feels like an antidepressant, with no major purpose or reason to really exist.- why and how will these objects be important? why should I as the reader care about it?</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: Discovered in a lab of an unknown GoI. Consists of an air freshener, a room that can only be accessed by smelling the content of the air freshener, and a lavender plant contained inside the room. The objects manifest anomalous anti-depressive and anti-memetic properties, since subjects exposed to any of the XXXX objects will manifest a general feeling of well-being; but they won't remember any initial exposition. Audiovisual data stored in a digital medium will experience total corruption and will be intelligible, however, textual and analogue data storage doesn't seem to be affected.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Currently this is closer to additional info on the skip than a narrative, there isn't anything that really expands on the setup or shows what happens during the story. How does the Foundation find everything out? (assuming characters are included) how are they affected? What is everything building up to? whats the conflict? whats the main focus or theme?</p>
<p>The story still feels relatively unfinished as there isn't much to go off other than the setup. I'm not sure what direction the story will take, or how all of this will fit into a narrative. (from what I can see here (and the sandbox)) the narrative looks like it will be mostly based around the effects, which alone won't be able to carry the article as they don't offer much to the audience without any purpose or context.</p>
<p><strong>Hook:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hook/Attention-Grabber: There's a fourth object which is only known to high clearance ranks. It's a virus that is presumably the cause of major depressive disorder and it's linked to the human subconscious since unknown times. Cannot be cured with any current means and it's symptoms can only be diminished with antidepressants. Also, one of the three initial objects has a very high value to the Foundation; it's a Thaumiel, and has a very high possibility to counterattack XXXX-4. Linked to SCP-5000 narrative (or at least I tried)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The '4th object' still isn't really present in the concept or narrative, I'm not sure why its important or what role it plays. Along with this, I wouldn't recommend having the object cause an incurable depression, in the context of this it just feels like its being used as an effect.</p>
<p>-Quick second note, the skip should be able to stand on its own without needing to rely on other articles. I would highly recommend writing this as its own story as cross-linking can be quite hard to pull off.</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight.</p>
<p>- Overall the skip doesn't seem to have much behind the effects, which makes it feel a bit close to a &quot;thing that does a thing&quot;.- whats the skips in-universe reason for existing? whats its purpose? how will all of this fit into a narrative?</p>
<p>-Some general advice on building narratives: try to get an idea of the beginning and the end, if you know what direction the narrative will take, then (generally speaking) it'll be easier to create.</p>
<p>If you would like me to give any further feedback, don't hesitate to send a PM, and if your looking for additional critiques or different perspectives, then I would highly recommend the butterfly squad. :)</p>
</div>
